sam: Quick heads-up: customer hit 'Request is not a constructor' in a Vercel Edge lambda. They had a node fetch polyfill bundled into their app.

mira: Yeah this is the classic constructor identity collision. Bundled polyfills + edge runtime = two different Request/Response prototypes.

owen: Agreed. For the SDK we should: 1) provide a tiny sync helper that prefers globalThis.fetch (safe for bundlers), 2) provide an async resolver that dynamically imports undici only at runtime in node, 3) export a BasicFetch TS alias so we don't leak undici types.

tia: Example patterns to add to README (copy-paste):
```js
// sync preferable for bundles (no dynamic import)
function getGlobalFetch() { return typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function' ? globalThis.fetch : undefined; }
// async resolver for node
async function getFetchAsync() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  const mod = await import('undici');
  return mod.fetch;
}
```

sam: Also document "do not import undici/polyfill-global" for edge builds and add a tiny FAQ: ERR_REQUIRE_ESM -> show exports mapping for CJS consumers.

mira: For TS: `export type BasicFetch = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;` and accept ClientOptions.fetch?: BasicFetch. Keeps d.ts stable across environments.

owen: I'll add a small sample-app: Next.js Edge + node CJS example and a Vite note about disabling node polyfills. That should reduce onboarding support.

tia: One more: add a duck-typed sanity check in SDK instead of instanceof. Quick util: `isFetchCompatible(f) => typeof f === 'function' && typeof f === 'function'` (very small).

